---
/**
 * About / methodology trust page (Phase 2D). Lifecycle: published_noindex.
 *
 * States independence, the source hierarchy, the FACT / INFERENCE / ESTIMATE discipline, the
 * expert-review policy, corrections, and the future contractor disclosure. It invents no
 * personnel, no credentials, and no legal copy (docs/04 → Expert Review, Disclosure;
 * /AGENTS.md §4.2).
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EvidenceNote from '../components/EvidenceNote.astro';
---

<BaseLayout pageId="about">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How this resource works</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Indy Sewer Resource exists to help Indianapolis-area homeowners make sewer decisions with the
      rules that actually apply to them. Its value depends entirely on being accurate about what is
      verified and honest about what is not, so the method is published alongside the content.
    </p>

    <h2 id="independent">Independent, and not a plumbing company</h2>
    <p>
      This site is independently owned and edited. It does not perform plumbing work, inspections, or
      repairs, and it is not affiliated with the City of Lawrence, Lawrence Utilities, or any other
      government body. Nothing here is a municipal notice, and nothing here replaces what your
      utility or a licensed contractor tells you about your property.
    </p>
    <p>
      No contractor currently sponsors, pays for, or influences anything on this site. The editorial
      decisions — what to cover, what to verify, what to leave out — stay with the publisher even if
      that changes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sources">Where the information comes from</h2>
    <p>Sources are used in a fixed order of preference:</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>the municipality's own ordinance or code;</li>
      <li>the utility or public works department's published policy and standards;</li>
      <li>engineering specifications issued for that jurisdiction;</li>
      <li>state or federal government sources;</li>
      <li>recognized technical standards and manufacturer documentation;</li>
      <li>credible contractor evidence;</li>
      <li>reputable secondary research.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A contractor's marketing page is never authoritative for a municipal or regulatory claim: if a
      rule matters, it has to come from the ordinance, the utility, or a specification. Credible
      contractor evidence can still support a narrower, appropriately scoped point where no
      primary or technical source covers it — and where it is used, it is identified as contractor
      evidence and its limitations are stated.
    </p>
    <p>
      For every source we record the document, the publisher, the date we verified it, and the
      limitation that applies — a superseded revision, a scanned PDF, an archived copy of a page the
      city has since removed, or a specification written for a different jurisdiction. Those
      limitations are printed on the page, not hidden in a footnote.
    </p>

    <h2 id="claims">Verified, reasoned, and unknown</h2>
    <p>
      Every material claim is classified behind the scenes as verified fact, inference, or estimate, and
      recorded that way in the page's publication record. On the page itself we show the label where it
      helps you judge what you are reading — particularly for reasoning and for gaps. What never happens
      is stating something as true when it has not been verified.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="How statements are classified">
      <table>
        <caption>How claims are classified.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Label</th>
            <th scope="col">What it means</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Verified (fact)</th>
            <td>Checked against a cited source that you can open and read yourself.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Inference</th>
            <td>
              A conclusion drawn from more than one source, with the reasoning shown so you can judge
              it. An inference is never presented as a fact.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Estimate</th>
            <td>
              A modeled figure with its basis and uncertainty stated. This site publishes no cost
              estimates today, because it has no evidence for them.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Not established</th>
            <td>
              A question we could not answer from the public record. It is named as a gap rather than
              filled with a plausible-sounding answer.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The last category is the one most sites omit. A permit fee we have not confirmed, or a
      responsibility boundary the ordinance does not settle, is written down as an open question and
      pointed at the office that can answer it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="expert-review">Review by a qualified professional</h2>
    <p>
      Pages that could materially change a homeowner's decision — repair-method guidance, whether a
      method suits a given line, responsibility determinations, and cost guidance — require review by
      a genuinely qualified professional before they are published to search engines. The preference
      is an Indiana-licensed plumber with trenchless and CIPP experience, or another real sewer
      rehabilitation professional.
    </p>

    <EvidenceNote kind="unverified" label="Current status">
      <p>
        <strong>No reviewer has reviewed this site yet.</strong> We will not invent a reviewer, a
        credential, a quote, or a review date to fill the gap. Until a named professional has actually
        reviewed it, the Lawrence resource stays a draft that is not published to search engines. When
        a review happens, the reviewer's name, relevant credential, what they reviewed, and the date
        will appear on the page — with their consent, and with any contractor relationship disclosed.
      </p>
    </EvidenceNote>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections and updates</h2>
    <p>
      Ordinances get amended, policy manuals get revised, and cities move documents. Sources are
      re-verified before a page is submitted for indexing and on a recurring schedule after that, and
      every page carries the date its sources were last checked.
    </p>
    <p>
      If something here is wrong or out of date, the correction is made on the page rather than
      quietly deleted, and material corrections are noted. Being publicly wrong about a rule that
      costs someone money is the worst outcome this project can produce, so accuracy takes priority
      over publishing quickly.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ai">How this content is produced</h2>
    <p>
      AI tools assist with research organization, drafting, and code. They do not substitute for
      evidence, for reading the primary sources, for professional review, or for editorial judgment,
      and no statement of fact enters a page because a model produced it. Anything that cannot be
      traced to a source is removed or labeled as unestablished.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contractors">Contractor relationships, if and when they exist</h2>
    <p>
      The long-term plan for this resource is to connect homeowners with one qualified trenchless
      contractor, who would pay for that access. That relationship does not exist today.
    </p>
    <p>If it ever does:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>it will be disclosed on the page where it applies, and at the point where contact happens;</li>
      <li>editorial control stays with the publisher — a sponsoring contractor does not get to edit, remove, or soften what the municipal rules say;</li>
      <li>if a reviewer is affiliated with that contractor, the affiliation will be stated alongside the review.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="privacy">Your information</h2>
    <p>
      This site collects no personal information. There is no contact form, no quote request, no
      newsletter signup, and no chat widget, and no homeowner data has ever been collected. If contact
      is added later, a privacy policy and a clear explanation of what is shared with a contractor —
      reviewed by a lawyer, not drafted here — will be published before any form goes live.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
